    "slug": "licensing-nj",
    "title": "How contractor licensing works in New Jersey",
    "scope": "state",
    "scopeKey": "nj",
    "body": "New Jersey regulates contractors more tightly than most states, and the rules run through a single agency: the Division of Consumer Affairs in the Department of Law and Public Safety. If you know which credential to look for and where to look it up, you can vet most of a contractor's paperwork in about ten minutes.\n\n## Who needs what credential\n\nFor general remodeling work — kitchens, baths, decks, roofing, siding, additions — New Jersey has long required contractors to register with the Division of Consumer Affairs under the Contractors' Registration Act. That system is now converting to true licensure: P.L. 2023, c.237 (signed January 8, 2024) creates the **State Board of Home Improvement and Home Elevation Contractors** within Consumer Affairs. The new license requires passing an examination; completing an apprenticeship, a trade-school program, or two years of supervised experience; carrying insurance; and posting $100,000 in security — a bond, letter of credit, or securities. The Board's education and training standards take effect February 1, 2026, and the general licensing requirement takes effect six months after the Board adopts its rules; contractors already registered are grandfathered into the new system. Until the switchover completes, verify the DCA registration; after it, verify the Board license. Either way, the contractor must hold a current credential from Consumer Affairs and display the number on contracts, invoices, and advertising.\n\nThe skilled trades are licensed separately, each by its own state board inside Consumer Affairs:\n\n- Electrical work: the Board of Examiners of Electrical Contractors\n- Plumbing: the State Board of Examiners of Master Plumbers\n- Heating and cooling: the State Board of Examiners of Heating, Ventilating, Air Conditioning and Refrigeration Contractors\n\nNew home builders are a different category again — they register with the Department of Community Affairs under the New Home Warranty program, not with Consumer Affairs.\n\n## How to verify a credential yourself\n\nUse the Division of Consumer Affairs' online license and registration verification search at njconsumeraffairs.gov. Search by business name or credential number and confirm three things: the credential is active, the business name matches the name on your contract exactly, and the credential type matches the work (an HVAC license does not cover electrical work). For a new home builder, check the builder registration through the Department of Community Affairs instead.\n\n## How permits work\n\nNew Jersey uses a single statewide building code, the Uniform Construction Code (UCC), adopted and updated by the Department of Community Affairs. Enforcement is local: every municipality has a construction code office (or shares one) that issues permits, collects fees, and sends inspectors. Structural changes, electrical, plumbing, HVAC replacements, and most significant alterations need a permit; purely cosmetic work like painting and flooring generally does not. Your town's construction official can tell you in one phone call whether a project needs one. Inspections happen at set stages, and the job is not legally complete until the final approval is issued.\n\n## Three practical hiring tips for New Jersey\n\n1. **Match the number to the name.** New Jersey requires home improvement contractors to put their Consumer Affairs number on all contracts and ads. Run that exact number through the state's verification search and make sure it belongs to the business entity you are paying — not a former employer or an unrelated company.\n2. **Insist on a compliant written contract.** For home improvement jobs over $500, state rules require a written contract with start and completion dates, the total price, and notice of your three-business-day right to cancel. A contractor who resists putting those terms in writing is telling you something.\n3. **Let the contractor pull the permit, in the contractor's name.** Under the UCC the permit applicant takes responsibility for the work. A contractor who asks you to take out a homeowner's permit for work they are performing is often avoiding scrutiny of their credentials — treat it as a red flag and call your municipal construction office before signing.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n- NJ Division of Consumer Affairs notice, \"HIC-HEC Regarding Changes To The Law, New Requirements For Registration\": https://www.njconsumeraffairs.gov/Documents/HIC-HEC-Regarding-Changes-To-The-Law-New-Requirements-For-Registration.pdf\n- P.L. 2023, c.237 (Home Improvement and Home Elevation Contractor Licensing Act): https://pub.njleg.state.nj.us/Bills/2022/PL23/237_.PDF\n- NJ State Board of Home Improvement and Home Elevation Contractors: https://www.njconsumeraffairs.gov/HIHEC\n\n*Drafted with AI assistance; reviewed by the LicensedList editorial team. Registration or licensure is not an endorsement of a business's competency.*",
    "author": "LicensedList editorial team",
