    "slug": "licensing-ny",
    "title": "How contractor licensing works in New York",
    "scope": "state",
    "scopeKey": "ny",
    "body": "New York is the trickiest of the three states for homeowners, because there is no statewide contractor license. Whether your contractor needs a license — and who issues it — depends almost entirely on where the house is.\n\n## A patchwork by design\n\nNew York State does not license general contractors or home improvement contractors at the state level. Instead, licensing happens locally:\n\n- **New York City (all five boroughs):** home improvement contractors must be licensed by the Department of Consumer and Worker Protection (DCWP, formerly the Department of Consumer Affairs). Separately, the NYC Department of Buildings licenses the construction trades — master plumbers, master fire suppression contractors, and electricians — and registers general contractors who file permits.\n- **Suburban counties:** Nassau, Suffolk, Westchester, Rockland, and Putnam counties each run their own consumer-affairs licensing programs for home improvement contractors. Some towns and cities layer their own requirements on top.\n- **Much of upstate:** many municipalities require no contractor license at all, which makes verifying insurance and references even more important.\n\nStatewide, the professions that touch construction — architects and professional engineers — are licensed by the New York State Education Department's Office of the Professions. Asbestos and some specialty work fall under state Department of Labor certification.\n\n## How to verify a credential yourself\n\nWhere the work is in New York City, use DCWP's online license check on nyc.gov to confirm a home improvement contractor's license, and the Department of Buildings' online tools to confirm a licensed plumber or electrician. In the suburban counties, call or search the county consumer affairs office that issued the license (for example, Nassau County Consumer Affairs or the Suffolk County Department of Labor, Licensing and Consumer Affairs). For architects and engineers, use the Office of the Professions verification search at op.nysed.gov. Always confirm the license is active and the name matches your contract.\n\n## How permits work\n\nNew York City runs its own construction code, and permits go through the NYC Department of Buildings — most filings now move through the DOB NOW online portal, and significant work must be filed by a registered design professional or licensed tradesperson. Outside the city, construction is governed by the New York State Uniform Fire Prevention and Building Code, overseen by the Department of State's Division of Building Standards and Codes but enforced by local town, village, and city building departments. That local office issues the building permit, performs inspections, and signs off with a certificate of occupancy or compliance. Requirements and turnaround times vary noticeably from one town to the next, so a quick call to the building department before you sign a contract is time well spent.\n\n## Three practical hiring tips for New York\n\n1. **Match the license to the jurisdiction.** A Westchester County license does not authorize work in Yonkers without the city's own license, and no suburban license covers work in the five boroughs. Verify the contractor is licensed where your property actually sits.\n2. **In NYC, expect two layers.** For a renovation involving plumbing or electrical, you should see both a DCWP-licensed home improvement contractor and DOB-licensed tradespeople on the job. Ask who is filing the DOB permit and confirm their license before work begins.\n3. **Where no license is required, verify insurance instead.** In unlicensed jurisdictions, ask for certificates of general liability and workers' compensation naming you as certificate holder, sent directly from the insurer or broker — not a photocopy from the contractor.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n- https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/index.page\n- https://dos.ny.gov/building-standards-and-codes\n- https://www.op.nysed.gov/\n\n*Drafted with AI assistance; reviewed by the LicensedList editorial team. Registration or licensure is not an endorsement of a business's competency.*",
    "author": "LicensedList editorial team",
