    "slug": "licensing-pa",
    "title": "How contractor licensing works in Pennsylvania",
    "scope": "state",
    "scopeKey": "pa",
    "body": "Pennsylvania sits between New Jersey's tight statewide system and New York's local patchwork. The state registers home improvement contractors but licenses very few trades itself — so a Pennsylvania homeowner needs to check both the state registry and the local rules.\n\n## Registration with the Attorney General\n\nUnder the Home Improvement Consumer Protection Act (HICPA), most contractors who perform home improvement work in Pennsylvania must register with the Office of Attorney General. Registered contractors receive a PA number (for example, PA012345) that must appear on contracts, estimates, and advertising. HICPA also sets contract rules: home improvement contracts over $500 must be in writing, include the registration number and insurance details, and give the homeowner a three-business-day right to rescind.\n\nOne caveat the Attorney General's office itself emphasizes: **registration is not an endorsement**. HICPA registration means the contractor has disclosed required information and carries minimum insurance — it does not mean the state has examined their skill, and it is not a statement about the quality of their work. Verifying registration is the floor, not the ceiling, of your due diligence.\n\n## Trade licensing is mostly local\n\nPennsylvania does not issue statewide licenses for electricians, plumbers, or general contractors. Those trades are licensed at the municipal level, and requirements vary widely — Philadelphia, Pittsburgh, Scranton, and many boroughs and townships have their own trade licensing, while other municipalities have none. In Philadelphia, the Department of Licenses and Inspections (L&I) licenses contractors, electrical contractors, plumbers, and related trades, and you can confirm a Philadelphia license through L&I's public records. A handful of specialties are state-licensed through the Department of State's professional licensing boards (searchable at pals.pa.gov), including crane operators, and architects and engineers.\n\n## How to verify a credential yourself\n\nStart with the Attorney General's public HICPA search at hicsearch.attorneygeneral.gov — enter the contractor's name or PA number and confirm the registration is current and matches the business name on your contract. Then check the local layer: for Philadelphia work, confirm the contractor and any trade subs hold active L&I licenses; elsewhere, ask your municipal code office which local licenses apply. For state-licensed professions, use the PALS search at pals.pa.gov.\n\n## How permits work\n\nPennsylvania adopted a statewide Uniform Construction Code (UCC), but enforcement is municipal: most townships and boroughs administer it through their own code office or a contracted third-party agency, and they issue the building, electrical, plumbing, and mechanical permits for your project. In Philadelphia, permits run through the Department of Licenses and Inspections, largely via its online portal. Structural work, additions, and most electrical and plumbing changes need a permit; your municipal code official can confirm what your project requires. Inspections occur at rough and final stages, and keeping the approved permit card and final approvals matters at resale.\n\n## Three practical hiring tips for Pennsylvania\n\n1. **Treat the PA number as a starting point.** Verify it at hicsearch.attorneygeneral.gov, but remember the Attorney General's caveat — registration is not a competency endorsement, so check references, insurance certificates, and local licenses too.\n2. **Ask the code office before you sign.** Because enforcement is municipal, call your township, borough, or Philadelphia L&I and ask what permits and local licenses your project needs. A reputable contractor's plan will match the answer.\n3. **Watch the HICPA contract requirements.** Missing registration number, missing insurance information, vague dates, or pressure to skip the written contract on a larger job are all HICPA compliance failures — and reliable early warnings about how the rest of the project will go.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n- https://www.attorneygeneral.gov/\n- https://hicsearch.attorneygeneral.gov/\n- https://www.phila.gov/departments/department-of-licenses-and-inspections/\n\n*Drafted with AI assistance; reviewed by the LicensedList editorial team. Registration or licensure is not an endorsement of a business's competency.*",
    "author": "LicensedList editorial team",
    "aiAssisted": true,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-24",
    "updatedAt": "2026-08-24"
