    "title": "What moves prices in Bergen County",
    "scope": "county",
    "scopeKey": "nj/bergen-county",
    "body": "Bergen County pricing reflects density and variety. The housing stock runs from prewar colonials and Tudors in towns like Ridgewood, Tenafly, and Rutherford — with plaster walls, aging cast-iron plumbing, and knob-and-tube surprises — to postwar splits and capes farther out, so two similar-sounding jobs can involve very different labor. With seventy municipalities, each running its own construction code office, permit timelines and inspection habits shift town by town, and contractors price that administrative overhead in. Tight lots and busy commuter streets complicate dumpster placement and material staging. Proximity to the George Washington Bridge keeps the labor market deep but also keeps crews in demand from both sides of the Hudson, and spring-through-fall exterior season books up early.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n- https://www.njconsumeraffairs.gov/\n- https://www.nj.gov/dca/\n\n*Drafted with AI assistance; reviewed by the LicensedList editorial team. Registration or licensure is not an endorsement of a business's competency.*",
