    "scope": "county",
    "scopeKey": "nj/essex-county",
    "body": "Essex County splits into two very different job sites. In Newark, East Orange, and Irvington, contractors work on dense blocks of prewar multifamily housing — two- and three-family frames with old wiring, shared walls, and tight rear yards that complicate staging. In Montclair, Glen Ridge, and Maplewood, the work shifts to large Victorians and center-hall colonials, often inside locally designated historic districts where exterior changes face review and matching old millwork or slate adds skilled hours. Street parking rules and narrow driveways affect delivery logistics almost everywhere. The county sits in a deep labor market shared with New York crews, and demand from the commuter-town renovation wave keeps the better firms scheduling months out, especially in spring.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n- https://www.njconsumeraffairs.gov/\n- https://www.nj.gov/dca/\n\n*Drafted with AI assistance; reviewed by the LicensedList editorial team. Registration or licensure is not an endorsement of a business's competency.*",
