    "body": "Queens is the most varied borough, and pricing varies with it. The stock runs from prewar co-op complexes in Jackson Heights and Forest Hills — where board approvals, alteration agreements, and building work rules shape every job — to brick semi-detached rows in Middle Village and Ridgewood, to postwar detached capes and colonials in Bayside and Whitestone that feel almost suburban. Attached and semi-attached homes limit side access, and parking pressure varies sharply by neighborhood, which changes delivery and dumpster logistics. All work runs through the NYC Department of Buildings, with design-professional filings adding cost and lead time to structural jobs. The borough's enormous, multilingual contractor base keeps competition healthy, though licensed trades stay in demand across the city.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n- https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/index.page\n- https://www.nyc.gov/site/buildings/index.page\n\n*Drafted with AI assistance; reviewed by the LicensedList editorial team. Registration or licensure is not an endorsement of a business's competency.*",
