    "title": "What moves prices in New York County",
    "scope": "county",
    "scopeKey": "ny/new-york-county",
    "body": "In New York County — Manhattan — the building itself often costs more to navigate than the renovation is complicated. Most work happens inside co-ops and condos, where board approval packages, alteration agreements, insurance requirements, and summer-only work rules add weeks before starting. Freight elevator reservations, protection of common areas, and restricted work hours slow production, and every sheet of drywall arrives through a loading dock on a scheduled window. Prewar buildings bring plaster, steel, and risers shared with neighbors; landmark districts add facade review. Department of Buildings filings require licensed professionals, and the trades that know high-end apartment work command the city's busiest calendars. Demand is effectively year-round, with board schedules — not weather — driving timing.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n- https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/index.page\n- https://www.nyc.gov/site/buildings/index.page\n\n*Drafted with AI assistance; reviewed by the LicensedList editorial team. Registration or licensure is not an endorsement of a business's competency.*",
